<Layouts.page title={@page_title} label="privacy.txt">
  <h1>MultiTunnelVPN Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p>
    This application does not collect or store personal data, contains no analytics or
    advertising, and never sends anything to the developer. Your settings, WireGuard
    profiles, and tailnet sign-in are stored on your device. WireGuard keys are kept in the
    device's keychain and are never included in backups; your other settings and tailnet
    sign-in are included in your device's backups, if you make them. Connection logs are off
    by default; if you turn them on, they are kept only in memory on your device and are
    erased when the VPN stops or you turn them off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Traffic sent to the developer loopback address is reflected back to your device and
    never leaves it.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the tailnet is turned on, your device connects to the coordination server you
    choose (Tailscale by default). That server receives the information needed to connect
    your devices, such as device keys, the device name set in this application, operating
    system, and network addresses. Your device also contacts that operator's relay servers,
    which see your IP address and pass along encrypted traffic when two devices can't
    connect directly. Signing in opens the operator's website. The operator's privacy
    policy applies. This application asks Tailscale not to collect client logs and never
    uploads logs.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a WireGuard profile is turned on, your internet traffic is sent, encrypted, to
    the server in that profile's configuration, and that provider's privacy policy applies.
  </p>
  <p>
    When DNS filtering is turned on, the domain names your apps look up are checked against
    blocklists on your device, and blocked lookups are answered on the device. Other
    lookups go to your VPN profile's resolver while it is on (if you allow that), and
    otherwise to the resolver you choose, Cloudflare (1.1.1.1) by default, under that
    resolver's privacy policy. Names in your tailnet are always looked up through your
    tailnet's DNS. While the VPN is connected, the blocklists you have turned on, and any
    you add yourself, are downloaded about once a day from the servers that host them
    (such as their publishers' sites, GitHub, and jsDelivr), which see your IP address like
    any website you visit. Lists you have not turned on are never downloaded, and nothing is
    downloaded while DNS filtering is off. Only running counts of lookups are kept, in
    memory, and no record of the names looked up is kept.
  </p>
  <p>
    To remove your data, delete your VPN profiles and sign out of your tailnet accounts in
    the application, then delete the application. Data held by your tailnet's coordination
    server, VPN provider, or DNS resolver is covered by their own privacy policies.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you installed this application from the App Store, then Apple does collect some
    personal data. Please see Apple's privacy policy here:
    <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" target="_blank">
      https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/
    </a>
  </p>
</Layouts.page>
